# Line-ending policy for the Merchandising System.
#
# Why this file exists, in one sentence: core.autocrlf is true on this machine and
# without a .gitattributes git decides line endings by guesswork, which silently
# breaks any file whose first line has to be exactly "#!/bin/sh".
#
# The git pre-commit hook (.git/hooks/pre-commit) is a /bin/sh script. If its
# shebang ever ends CRLF, sh looks for an interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r",
# fails, and THE HOOK STOPS RUNNING WITHOUT SAYING ANYTHING. That hook is the only
# enforcement layer that catches edits made in Visual Studio 2026, which bypass the
# Claude Code hooks entirely (CLAUDE.md section 11). A silent failure there is the
# worst outcome in the whole guardrail arrangement.

* text=auto

# Shell and SQL must be LF. sh will not tolerate CR, and MariaDB migrations are
# checksummed by the P1-06 runner - a line-ending flip would change every checksum
# and make the runner refuse to proceed on a schema that had not actually changed.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf

# Windows-side source. CRLF keeps Visual Studio 2026 and the WPF designer from
# rewriting whole files on save and producing diffs that are pure noise.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf
*.vb      text eol=crlf
*.vbproj  text eol=crlf
*.sln     text eol=crlf
*.xaml    text eol=crlf
*.config  text eol=crlf
*.props   text eol=crlf
*.targets text eol=crlf

# CAUTION on *.ps1 eol=crlf: scripts/install-hooks.ps1 embeds the pre-commit hook
# body as a here-string. Once that file is checked out with CRLF, the here-string
# carries CRLF too, and a naive writer would emit a CRLF shebang - reintroducing
# exactly the failure this file prevents. install-hooks.ps1 therefore normalises
# the hook body to LF and writes bytes directly. Do not "simplify" it back to a
# plain Set-Content.

# Binary - never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.pfx binary
*.snk binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
